<section class="mp-section" id="responsibilities"><div class="mp-wrap"><div class="mp-heading"><p class="mp-kicker">01 / Make the responsibility visible</p><h2>Know where the tools help<br>and where a person decides.</h2><p>Ask who will make the calls, review the message and decide whether a meeting qualifies. Those responsibilities define a human-led service.</p></div><p class="gp-scroll-hint">Scroll horizontally to review the responsibility table on smaller screens.</p>
<div class="gp-table-wrap" role="region" aria-labelledby="human-role-caption" tabindex="0"><table class="gp-table"><caption id="human-role-caption">Responsibilities in a CallTeam human-led appointment-setting campaign</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Campaign task</th><th scope="col">Where technology can assist</th><th scope="col">What people own</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Account preparation</th><td>Organizing records, public research and possible buying signals.</td><td>Account fit, source review, exclusions and a defensible reason to call.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Message preparation</th><td>Drafting and organizing relevant product or account information.</td><td>Approved claims, useful questions and the language used with buyers.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Live outreach</th><td>Calling workflow, accessible contact records and agreed activity logging.</td><td>The spoken conversation, listening, objection handling and redirection.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Qualification</th><td>Presenting agreed fields and organizing notes.</td><td>Testing the premise, recording confirmed facts and deciding whether the next step fits.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Scheduling and handoff</th><td>Calendar availability, reminders and CRM workflow.</td><td>Agreed meeting purpose, relevant participants, useful context and ownership.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Campaign review</th><td>Activity summaries and reporting views.</td><td>Interpreting responses, reviewing seller feedback and approving changes.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p>AI-assisted information is preparation, not proof of intent. A new executive or technology project can justify a question. Only the conversation can establish what the prospect confirms. Our <a href="/ai-lead-generation-services/">AI lead generation services</a> explain the account-research layer.</p></div></section>

<section class="mp-section mp-paper" id="delivery-models"><div class="mp-wrap"><div class="mp-heading"><p class="mp-kicker">02 / Compare the job, not the label</p><h2>AI SDR versus human SDR:<br>who does the work?</h2><p>AI SDR is used for different products. Separate email assistance, autonomous voice workflows and a managed human team before comparing a price or claimed output.</p></div>
<div class="gp-table-wrap" role="region" aria-labelledby="human-model-caption" tabindex="0"><table class="gp-table"><caption id="human-model-caption">Three delivery models to evaluate against your actual sales workflow</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">What may perform the outreach</th><th scope="col">What the buying team should establish</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">AI email assistant</th><td>Software helps research, write, send or classify email, depending on the product.</td><td>Who reviews claims, handles replies, qualifies interest and performs any live calls.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">AI voice-agent system</th><td>A configured voice system can conduct calls, collect information and book appointments.</td><td>Approved use cases, human escalation, review, contact controls and responsibility for incorrect outputs.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Managed human calling</th><td>People speak with prospects, supported by research and workflow tools.</td><td>Assigned callers, training, supervision, qualification, follow-up and the information handed to sales.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p>These categories describe delivery, not a universal performance ranking. A human service may suit complex questions and live qualification; an automated workflow still needs an owner and a suitable use case. Review the full <a href="/services/b2b-appointment-setting/">CallTeam appointment-setting service</a> for the broader commercial scope.</p></div></section>

<section class="mp-section" id="live-decisions"><div class="mp-wrap"><div class="mp-heading"><p class="mp-kicker">03 / The work inside the conversation</p><h2>A useful caller knows<br>when to change direction.</h2><p>The following are illustrative decisions, not client transcripts. Each shows the judgment and record a live conversation can require.</p></div><div class="gp-grid-two">
<article class="gp-tile"><span class="gp-route">The premise is wrong</span><h3>“That project is already finished.”</h3><p>Correct the account note. Ask one relevant question only if another need falls within the offer. Do not continue presenting a public signal as a current buying project.</p></article>
<article class="gp-tile"><span class="gp-route">The role is different</span><h3>“Another team owns this.”</h3><p>Clarify the responsible function and whether a referral is appropriate. Carry useful context forward so the next contact starts from the right question.</p></article>
<article class="gp-tile"><span class="gp-route">Interest needs context</span><h3>“Send something first.”</h3><p>Establish which question the information should answer and agree suitable follow-up. A polite response alone does not make an appointment qualified.</p></article>
<article class="gp-tile"><span class="gp-route">The account should stop</span><h3>“Remove us from the campaign.”</h3><p>Record the request and apply the agreed suppression process. The next action is a record update, not another attempt to secure the meeting.</p></article>
</div><p class="mp-caption">Review contact rules, approved claims and suppression responsibilities before launch. The <a href="/articles/b2b-cold-calling-compliance-global/">B2B calling compliance guide</a> provides the wider planning framework.</p></div></section>

<section class="mp-section mp-paper" id="handoff"><div class="mp-wrap mp-split"><div><p class="mp-kicker">04 / Make the first sales conversation easier</p><h2>Your salesperson needs<br>more than a calendar slot.</h2><p>Agree the meeting standard before calling starts. Company fit and a relevant role matter, but your salesperson also needs the reason for the appointment and the questions it should answer.</p><p>Capture the current process, confirmed interest, stakeholders, timing and next action at the depth the conversation supports. Leave missing budget, authority or technical facts open for discovery instead of turning assumptions into qualification.</p><p>CRM access, field names, confirmation work and the receiving owner belong in the scope. Your sellers then return attendance, acceptance and opportunity feedback so the campaign can learn from the handoff.</p><a class="mp-button" href="/b2b-meeting-quality-check/">Check Your Meeting Quality ↗</a></div>
<aside class="mp-note"><p class="mp-label">Illustrative sales handoff</p><h3>A reason to meet.<br>A clear next question.</h3><dl><div><dt>Buyer</dt><dd>Operations leader responsible for a multi-site service process.</dd></div><div><dt>Confirmed</dt><dd>Separate locations use different tracking methods.</dd></div><div><dt>Purpose</dt><dd>Review whether the proposed workflow can support consistent reporting.</dd></div><div><dt>Open questions</dt><dd>IT requirements and the budget owner need discovery.</dd></div><div><dt>Next action</dt><dd>Your seller reviews the workflow with the operations lead.</dd></div></dl><p class="mp-small">Fictional example of a usable brief. It is not a reported campaign result.</p></aside></div>
<div class="mp-wrap"><div class="mp-tool-band"><div><h3>Agree the calling work for your market.</h3><p>Use the campaign planner to connect your offer, audience and conversation approach.</p></div><a class="mp-button" href="/b2b-outbound-campaign-planner/">See How We’d Run Your Campaign ↗</a></div></div></section>

<section class="mp-section mp-proof" id="campaign-proof"><div class="mp-wrap"><div class="mp-heading"><p class="mp-kicker">05 / Examine actual campaign evidence</p><h2>Read what happened<br>and how the work was done.</h2><p>These published results show the context of individual engagements. They do not establish a performance advantage over automation or another agency.</p></div><div class="gp-grid-two">
<article class="gp-stat-block"><p class="mp-label">B2B SaaS / 90-day outreach period</p><h3>11 qualified demos.<br>Two customers within six months.</h3><p>The case connects account selection, software conversations and qualification to the reported sales progression.</p><a href="/case-studies/saas-demo-appointment-setting/">Read the SaaS appointment-setting case →</a></article>
<article class="gp-stat-block"><p class="mp-label">Workforce software / first 90 days</p><h3>Nine booked demos.<br>Two proposals.</h3><p>The workforce software case reports those stages separately. Attendance and closed-customer figures are outside its reported result.</p><a href="/case-studies/hr-software-lead-generation/">Read the HR software campaign case →</a></article>
</div><p class="mp-proof-note">Client identities are withheld. Results depend on the offer, audience, data, delivery and sales follow-through. For a software-specific engagement, explore <a href="/services/hr-tech-lead-generation/">HR tech lead generation and appointment setting</a>.</p></div></section>

<section class="mp-section" id="scope"><div class="mp-wrap mp-split"><div><p class="mp-kicker">06 / People, scope and investment</p><h2>Know who will<br>run your campaign.</h2><p class="mp-price">From US$4,000<span> / month</span></p><p>The published starting scope assumes a campaign-ready offer, defined market, usable target records, CRM and calendar access, and a receiving sales team. Research, licensed data, more markets and expanded capacity affect the final fee.</p><p>Most programmes begin with three months. Confirm the assigned people, buyer coverage, approved message, contact controls and reporting in the proposal. <a href="/pricing/">Review pricing and the full starting assumptions</a>.</p><p>CallTeam handles the preparation, calling, follow-up and qualification agreed in scope. Your team supplies accurate product evidence, approvals and seller feedback, then owns discovery, proposals and closing.</p></div><aside class="mp-tool"><p class="mp-label">Look at the progression</p><h3>Separate activity<br>from useful outcomes.</h3><ul class="gp-checklist"><li><strong>Booked:</strong> a next conversation was scheduled.</li><li><strong>Held:</strong> the meeting actually took place.</li><li><strong>Accepted:</strong> sales confirms the agreed qualification standard.</li><li><strong>Opportunity:</strong> the receiving team records a real commercial next step.</li></ul><p>A service fee and a cost per qualified meeting describe different things. Use your complete cost and actual results when reviewing the campaign.</p></aside></div></section>
